A||||8||Don_Chi_Chi||Honesty please!||||1||
Z||000000||Don_Chi_Chi||10-24-2001||05:22 PM||||We all know that 2nd gen MR2's have advantages over the beloved fiero, but what exactly are they?  Handling?  Acceleration?  Has anyone ever put a 3rd gen Mr2 up against a fiero yet?<P>------------------<BR>Previously known as Saleenfiero||||reg||1
Z||000001||TRiAD||10-24-2001||05:27 PM||||The 2nd gen NA has 140HP, and should run very close to a stock GT. <P>The Turbo will run a 5 second 0-60 sprint...we'll be eating dust (again, stock). <P>The MR2 Spyder is again in the 140hp range, but heavier, 0-60 is about 7.3, and should be a bit easier to take (for now). <P>I honestly think stock-stock, the MR2s have us. <BR>In handling, the gap only gets bigger. <P>BUT, Fieros are a LOT less expensive, and for the pricetag on an MR2 Spyder, I can build a Fiero that will run with a Z06.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><BR><P>------------------<BR><B><I>Michael ~ triadtuning@hotmail.com</I></B><BR>-----------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88FieroGTwhitefscustomcroppedmini.jpg"> <BR><I>Photoshop rendering of planned exterior modifications.</I>||||reg||1
Z||000002||AusFiero||10-24-2001||05:43 PM||||I own a second generation MR2 and a Fiero GT Don so I could probably judge this for you. <BR>Handling - MR2 wins. Not a lot in it but noticeable.<BR>Braking - MR2 wins big time. Is a later model car so so it should. The ABS is the deciding factor and I don't feel scared stopping at high speed in the MR2.<BR>Acceleration - Much of a muchness. I was going to buy an MR2 turbo which kills Fieros but got a normally aspirated one for a steal of a price. Have since added a new exhaust system and a cold air induction system. Now it would beat my Fiero GT easily. <BR>Interior - MR2 again. But again it is a later model car so it just has that later model feel to it. My Fieros interior was custom so it looked pretty hot.<BR>Exterior looks - Fiero I think as it looks more Ferrari like. But the MR2 is nice too. Personal preference really.<BR>The Fiero in my opinion is a better car out on the open road. Quieter and has a touring feel to it. The MR2 is nippier and great around town. <P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero/ausfiero2.gif">    <BR><A HREF="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero" TARGET=_blank>My Fiero</A> | <A HREF="http://www.fieroshop.com.au" TARGET=_blank>Fiero Accessories</A>||202.139.20.3||reg||1
Z||000003||Don_Chi_Chi||10-24-2001||11:12 PM||||What year/model MR2 do you have Aus?  Any pics?<P>------------------<BR>Previously known as Saleenfiero||||reg||1
Z||000004||Slammed Fiero||10-24-2001||11:30 PM||||You will find the MR2 is a much more Nimble car than a Fiero. The Fiero's weight make it feel durable , and predictable at the limit.<P>Triad..I'm disappointed in you.. the MR2 Turbo does it in 5.99 Sec according to their old ad's. But as we all know turning up the juice on a turbo doesn't take much.<P>It's really like comparing an axe to a scalple. Both cars do the same thing , one does it with a lot more precision. <P>There both great cars in their own right. The Fiero is more of a Hotrod. Thats what I think draws people to it. I had a chance to Drive a prepped 88 Supercharged Mr2 at an autocross recently. Nimble car , a little twitchy at the limit , no more power than my 2.8 (I have raced the Mr2 with my Fiero past 100mph) The car was just plain easier to drive fast.<P>Just my 2 cents. I always liked the looks of the 2nd Gen Cars. The spyder kinda makes me think history is repeating itself , great chassis , ugly car (like Mk1 Mr2)<P>------------------<BR>Jonathan McCreery<BR>86 GT 4spd<BR>Koni's , Coilovers, Big Bars , 17" Trmotorsports Typhoons , 11.25" vented discs, Wilwood 4 Piston Calipers , Pirelli P Zero's, Short Shift, No cat , Cold air induction , Geni Stainless tips, White face gauges , Momo shift , momo pedals, Big Bore TB, 70Hp Shot of NOS Autocrossed , Tracked , Dragged? (soon) abused.||||reg||1
Z||000005||firstfiero||10-25-2001||10:49 AM||||      Thanks for the numbers. I was have a conversation recently and made the comment that an mr2 turbo is one of the best bangs for the buck out their. I new kid I just hired says their not fast and him and his friend beet one in a stock regular old neon. After I got off the floor from laughing I explained to him That I beet neons all day and wouldn't think of screwing with a turbo mr2. Then my friend chimes in that in his 92 non turbo 300 z he wouldn't beat on either but this kid insists and says that they beat a turbo eclipse also. We just ignored is ignorance after that but I wanted to get some numbers. Anybody have the numbers for an eclipse turbo...2nd gen<P>------------------<BR><BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/firstfiero.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Slammed Fiero||10-25-2001||11:07 AM||||I don't know the Numbers. i do know when the Mr2 Turbo came out it was faster to 60 than the same year NSX (5.9 to 6.3) The 300ZX TT of the same years wasn't much faster at mid 5's. Most magazines called the mr2 a Ferrari in 3/4 scale<P>As for bang for the buck. I don't know how true that is. The Mr2 was overpriced when it came out , and it holds it's value. There still around 17-18K Canadian for a good example.<P>I guess it all depends on what you want. There a great platform for mods. But my 17-18 Grand would go towards a Vette (c4)<P>Turbo eclipse.. AWD's are high 6's to 60 14.9's in the 1/4. But keep in mind Turbo = Cheap Hp. I had a ride in a low 12 sec 1/4 mile talon a few months back... Scary<P>You couldn't catch a cold with a neon. Let alone anything with a turbo.<P>JM||||reg||1
Z||000007||Voytek||10-25-2001||06:24 PM||||The Fiero is a lot less costly and won't have rust holes in 5 years (as long as you maintain the underside).<P>The MR2 gen. II is definitely a more modern car but virtually everyone I talk to prefers the looks of my '87 GT over an MR2.  Now, you COULD make a 2nd gen. MR2 look ALMOST as exotic as a Fiero by dressing it up.  Do you have an additional $4-5,000 to spend on the body?<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/voyteks_sig2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000008||mrfiero||10-25-2001||06:40 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Slammed Fiero:<BR><B><BR>Triad..I'm disappointed in you.. the MR2 Turbo does it in 5.99 Sec according to their old ad's. </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Actually, the early ads claimed 0-60 in 5.96 seconds.  This was achieved with no regard for the clutch or driveline.....they basically abused the heck out of it to produce a sub 6-sec. time.  Later ads had the time in the 6.2 second range, which was more realistic.  <P>I test drove a new '91 MR2 Turbo in the spring of 1990 (right after I bought my '88 GT).....it was definitely faster, but I still prefer an '88 Fiero GT.  It's all in your persoanl preferences.  Besides, I only paid $9000 for a 20K mile '88 GT (in 1990) and that MR2 Turbo was over $20K!  The GT made better financial sense at the time.<P>BTW......stock naturally aspirated MR2's are dogs.  A stock V-6 can take them fairly easily.  The upside to the MR2 over the Fiero is availability of aftermarket go-fast goodies.  There is a plethora of crap you can buy for an MR2 to make it much faster than a V-6 Fiero.<P>BTW.....all stock '91-'95 MR2 turbos run 200 HP. <p>[This message has been edited by mrfiero (edited 10-25-2001).]||||reg||1
